WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.190
- The words death, die, and dead

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.810
are all likely derived from
the proto Indo-European root

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.760
deu which meant to die, faint, or vanish.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.670
It's also where we get the word funeral,

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.120
but if you're actually at a funeral,

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.820
you probably wouldn't
use any of these words.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.823
It just feels too blunt
to say, sorry he's dead.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:21.180
When discussing topics that
are sensitive or taboo,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.500
people often employ euphemisms,

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.536
words or phrases that replace direct terms

00:00:25.536 --> 00:00:28.300
in order to spare feelings,
avoid embarrassment,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.190
or even out of superstition.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:32.700
And when it comes to
sensitive or taboo topics,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.974
nothing is as universally avoided,

00:00:34.974 --> 00:00:39.120
yet universally unavoidable, as death.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.790
So it's got a lot of euphemisms
in virtually every language.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.940
Whether you believe that
you're going somewhere

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.350
or getting reincarnated or
will just cease to exist,

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.240
you're going to have to
face your own mortality

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.210
at some point.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.890
So why not talk about it now?

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.700
Euphemistically, of course.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.123
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovksy,
and this is Otherwords.

00:00:58.092 --> 00:01:00.925
(whimsical music)

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.250
- Otherwords.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.700
- Perhaps the safest
way to talk about death

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.000
is to avoid the act of dying altogether

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.960
and instead focus on the person's absence.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.944
So you might say they're
gone or no longer with us

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:22.110
or in Mandarin, they're already not here.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:22.990
If you're religious,

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:24.800
you might attribute
that absence to a deity,

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.010
saying they are with the
Lord or summoned by God,

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.860
or had Allah take their soul.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.220
Many euphemisms frame death
as a journey or crossing.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.330
You might say someone passed,
departed, slipped away,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.330
or even sprouted wings.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.050
Some sayings even specify the destination.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:44.570
In China, you can fly on a
crane to the Western paradise.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.460
In America, you can ride to
the happy hunting grounds,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.720
a term for the afterlife

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.070
that has been attributed
to Native Americans,

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:54.100
but was more likely made
up by European colonists.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.010
You can meet St. Peter
at the Pearly Gates,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.930
or if you're into Greek mythology,

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.520
you can cross the river Styx,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.260
an underworld waterway where
souls would be ferried across

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.840
by the boatman, Charon.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.420
And if the deceased is a pet,

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.270
they might cross the rainbow bridge,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.080
perhaps an allusion to the Bifrost bridge

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.900
of Norse mythology.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.560
- This stupid dog won't die.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.000
- Doors, gates, rivers, and
bridges are common imagery,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.670
highlighting the belief in
a boundary between worlds,

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.180
which the deceased would
have to somehow penetrate.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.370
By the way, figures
like Charon or St. Peter

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:25.470
or the Grim Reaper,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.810
whose job is to guide
souls across that boundary

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.540
are known as psychopomps,

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.090
which is a pretty cool word,

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:34.150
and would also be a great name
for a Neo-rockabilly band.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.920
And speaking of rockabilly,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.260
another departure metaphor

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.840
can be traced to The King himself.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.700
To try to get the crowd to
go home after his concerts.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:43.690
announcers would tell them

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.840
that Elvis has left the building.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.850
Ever since he joined the Choir Invisible,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.890
"left the building" has become
a euphemism for dying.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.550
Slumber is another popular
metaphor for death,

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.350
which is not surprising since
it's the closest experience

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.370
in life we have to relate to.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.920
You can be laid to rest
or take the big sleep.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.720
In Afrikaans, you close
your eyes for the last time,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.220
fall into an eternal dream in Hungarian

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.720
and use the earth as
your blanket in Lakota.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.550
That sounds nice. I mean,
who doesn't like sleep?

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:14.540
On the other hand,

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.459
you can also take a dirt nap
or sleep with the fishes,

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:20.430
which sound decidedly less pleasant.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.490
These are examples of dysphemisms,

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.960
substitutions that are
deliberately blunt or insensitive.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.488
So euphemistically, someone
might breathe their last,

00:03:29.488 --> 00:03:32.530
but dysphemistically, that
old geezer down the road

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.030
finally bit the dust.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.740
There are a lot of dysphemisms for dying

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.420
like croaking or giving up the ghost.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.380
You might use them to celebrate
the expiration of someone

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.720
you didn't like,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.300
but they can also serve
a positive function.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.560
To give us a sense of
power over something scary

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.900
and confusing.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.120
After all, how can I
be afraid of carking it

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.020
when I talk about it so cavalierly?

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.240
A popular dysphemism for death in the UK

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.610
is popped his clogs,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.470
which means pawned his shoes

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.140
because he won't be needing them anymore

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.370
and funerals are expensive.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.060
A lot of death metaphors deal
with the things you won't need

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.840
or can't use.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:07.970
According to the Portuguese,

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.330
you won't be eating mangoes next season.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:11.930
You won't be eating anything in fact,

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.950
so you may as well stick
your spoon in the wall

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.680
and leave your teeth behind.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.010
You can also kick the calendar

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.460
because time is a living person's problem

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.900
and swallow your birth certificate

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.470
because age is irrelevant at this point.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.760
However, the Seediq people of Taiwan

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.990
hold out hope for one more meal

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.290
because their dead go to plant yams.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.080
But perhaps the most dysphemistic
ways of referring to death

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.670
involve the grave.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.370
Six feet under, bought a pine condo,

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:40.140
went into the fertilizer
business, food for worms,

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.138
even wearing wooden pajamas.

00:04:42.138 --> 00:04:45.240
These sayings ignore the
lofty journeys of the soul,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.610
and instead describe what happens

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.590
to your physical body after death.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.380
which let's be honest, isn't pretty.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.410
Some refer to the
position of the body like

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.400
belly up, toes up, or
stretching one's legs.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.960
A lot of languages focus on the flowers.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.270
Whether you're pushing
up daisies in English,

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.400
raising mallow in Spanish,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.810
looking at daisies from below in Dutch,

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.410
smelling flowers from
below in Hebrew and Polish

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.370
violets in particular in Hungarian

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.990
or eating dandelions
by the roots in French.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.440
These sayings might be oddly specific,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.290
but you could still sort
of guess their meaning

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.230
if you'd never heard them before.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.057
This is not true of idioms,

00:05:21.057 --> 00:05:24.110
which are phrases whose
origins are now so obscure

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:26.760
that they make no literal sense today.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.620
The most famous English idiom for death

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.750
is probably kick the bucket.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.270
Some suggests that this refers to

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.430
a particular method of hanging,

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.870
but it's more likely that
the bucket of this phrase

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.700
is an old term for the wooden beam

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.320
that an animal's feet would be
tied to for slaughtering.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.140
Quite a grizzly image to end
up on so many travel blogs.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.020
Another popular idiom is bought the farm.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.392
Again, a widespread and
probably incorrect explanation

00:05:52.392 --> 00:05:56.360
claims that test pilots in
World War One used this term

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.340
because if you crashed your
plane into a nearby field,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.250
the government would
reimburse the property owner

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.300
for the damage,

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.960
which could then be used
to pay off the mortgage,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:06.850
I.E. buy the farm,

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:08.380
but whenever the origin for an idiom

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:13.030
just sounds a little too
tidy, it probably isn't true.

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.610
In fact, referring to
death as buying a plot,

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.290
predates airplanes by several decades.

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.800
So the farm in question is more likely

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.520
just a metaphor for the grave,

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.260
which you purchase with your life.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.160
Buying the farm and popping your clogs

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.470
aren't the only sayings that
frame death as a transaction.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.450
You can also cash in your
chips, get your ticket punched,

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:34.180
and check out.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.290
Humans have long seen death as a time

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.270
for squaring up old debts.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.540
In ancient Greece,

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.740
mourners would place a coin
in the deceased's mouth

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:42.800
before burial,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.910
so they'd have money to pay the boatman.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.090
Hey, even psychopomps gotta make a living.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:49.720
It makes sense that we have
so many terms for death

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.790
because our feelings about
it are so complicated.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.220
One word was never going
to cover the vast array

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.660
of human emotions and experiences
that revolve around it.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.340
We may disagree about what happens

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.730
after we've sung our swan song.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.100
Perhaps we'll meet our maker

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.843
or maybe we're just plain

00:07:05.843 --> 00:07:08.730
brown bread, but at least the debate will be colorful.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.517
A popular dysphemism for death--

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.740
A popular dysphemism for death in the UK

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.173
is popped his clogs.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:19.758
A popular--

00:07:19.758 --> 00:07:21.280
(laughing)

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.403
I was too excited.

